hi it's been a while up until I released  the flying fantasy beasts video there'll  be a card on the screen summer and link  in the description this channel saw  something of a hiatus for a couple of  months as the title of the video  wherever it is suggests my thesis had a  lot to do with that there were other  factors I should point out as you'll see  in a vlog very shortly I went on holiday  for two weeks with some friends from  school we went to France so we could all  properly unplug from work was the first  time I've had a proper break since three  years ago and then for a week before  that I was volunteering with my old  Scout troop which was this amazing week  where I was a leader on a camp where  previously had been a scout and we were  teaching the kids how to make fires cook  on fires guts and prepare and cook  rabbits  we built a zipline on the campsite which  we did when we were kids and we went  coasteering  and generally we just gave the kids the  childhood that we had we the leaders had  when we were in that Scout troop which  was so fulfilling I actually wrote a  blog post about the experience because I  went without my phone or without  internet for a week which is the first  time item plug from social media  probably for like ten years so there's a  link to that in the description I have a  blog by the way however the main point  of this video is talking about thesis  stuff and specifically why I had to  submit my thesis again so the way that  you get a PhD is pretty simple  really you do original research for  however many years you write it up into  a thesis over several months or a year  and then you submit that thesis your  examined on it in a visor and then  you're either awarded a PhD or not  simple I mean obviously it's not and  there are also other steps which people  tend to gloss over in how a PhD is  awarded and specifically the steps after  you do your volva if you've watched this  channel for a while you might remember  that I did my advisor in February and I  did a video about the experience which  was both terrifying and enjoyable and  kind of a weird mix of emotions but I  passed the fiver I was awarded the PhD  subject to Corrections because the PhD  thesis that you submit for examination  in the Bible is the first version of  your PhD the way that the fiber works is  they basically say  yes but or no the but is the corrections  aspect to it there are two kinds of  Corrections at least in the UK system  those are major Corrections and minor  Corrections now minor Corrections is  often stuff basically just spelling  grammar all the way up to we want you to  restructure how the thesis is but we  don't want you to do any additional work  anything up to doing additional work is  basically classed as minor and that's  the kind of thing where you get a few  months to make those changes and then  you send it off to your internal  examiner they verify that you've done  the changes asked for and then boom  you're away you can graduate the second  kind of Corrections the major  Corrections are the ones that I've got  now the word major implies that  something was fundamentally wrong and  that's not actually the case I thought  that up until my fiverr what the  examiners stressed in my viber was they  were giving me major Corrections but  that was only because they wanted me to  do some additional work that was sort of  the necessary condition for major the  actual stuff that needed to go to the  PhD was only in the end a couple of  pages extra and I barely had to touch  the majority of it apart from a few  spelling mistakes here and there and a  few bits of cuts and a few equations to  tweak almost everything that I had to do  was actually very little space on the  page in the end the key point though is  that if you get given major Corrections  you have to do additional work on your  thesis and then resubmit it everybody  who gets Corrections has to resubmit  their thesis but if you get major  Corrections then there's usually a bit  more work involved than you have to do  extra stuff the original work plus the  extra stuff then forms the second form  of your PhD thesis which fingers crossed  gets accepted by your examiners and then  that's the final version so everybody  who does a PhD actually has a few steps  added on to the end of the process you  do your original work you write it up  you go through a fiver and then you have  to make changes to that thesis and then  you resubmit it so what I've been doing  for the past couple of months is that  extra work which turned out whilst it  wasn't a huge amount extra to do there  were so many problems to overcome in  actually doing it I want this to be a  relatively short video explaining where  I've been so when it go into the full  details but basically I had to do an  extra section of one of my chapters  which effectively verified what I'd done  in the rest of the thesis  since something I really should have  thought of before I submitted and I felt  kind of dumb in retrospect for not doing  it in the first place the problem was in  order to do that sort of verification  I needed to get another data set and it  was a data set that wasn't available  from the source that originally used  which was the ECMWF I had to find it  somewhere else  and what I did to begin with was do all  the extra work given a nearly identical  data set to what I needed because it was  the one I had access to from the ECMWF  however I under odd about it and I  wasn't happy I wasn't happy with doing  it with an approximated version I wanted  to get the actual completely accurate it  was basically trying to find a string  function rather than geopotential which  was depending on how you look at the  world isn't there's no difference but  there is a difference so I did all the  corrections work I then have a crisis of  confidence and then I went on this epic  adventure to try and find the data set  that I needed and that took quite a  while not the several months that I was  missing for though because eventually I  did find the data but it wasn't in the  nicest form previously when I'd used  geophysical data I was able to download  one file which had all the data that I  wanted from for example 1979 to 2012 and  it was a low resolution which is what  you use for the whole thesis it was  every six hours boom one giant file of  like 20 gigabytes however because this  new dataset came from a different  provider which was cedar actually based  in the same city I believe as the ECMWF  but different for bureaucracy reasons  data from this source wasn't available  in that one file form and that meant  that I had to download I think it was  about 250 different data files that  covered it for I think it was a month at  a time and then you had one month  another month another month motherland'  I couldn't back download them I  literally had to sit for a whole day was  like 18 hours clicking to download them  one after the other because my browser  would only accept so many and I couldn't  be bothered to write a program that was  just going to automatically download  them because you had to request it and  then download it and then wait for the  download and then anyway there were  problems and then when I had the data  there were still problems because it  turned out there were a few gaps in the  data  tonight it was on a different grid I  then had to be sample it in the vertical  and then eventually after about a month  I think it was of fiddling around with  this thing I eventually was able to redo  the work by down in the first place  and what did I find almost exactly the  same result which was a little bit  depressing but I knew that it was  exactly correct it wasn't their  approximation there wasn't any doubt I  knew that I had done exactly the right  thing and that was the most gratifying  thing about the process of doing the  corrections and resubmitting the thesis  it was that I knew what I was doing when  I did the thesis the first time around  it kind of I'm sure a lot of PhD  students would agree with me in this in  writing a thesis it feels kind of like a  series of stumbles one after the other  and eventually they just kind of acts  almost accidentally form a body of work  it's very rare that you'll come across  someone whose thesis was well defined  from the beginning to the end and it was  just a series of filling in the gaps  almost everybody's I swear is kind of  made up on the fly and adapted to  results that you find and new ideas that  you have and you know you might find  something that blows a whole section out  of the water and you have to replace it  and it ends up being this kind of  garbled Frankenstein's monster for  creation with the correction though I  had a really clear idea what I needed to  do I had a plan I followed it through  boom got the results interpreted them  wrote them up and I realized how much  the PhD had changed me because for the  first time in my academic career and by  that I mean my master's on my PhD I  wasn't feeling the need to verify what  I'd done with my supervisor I did send  the results to my supervisor and just  made sure that the corrections sort of  the interpretation of the results was  what they expect which was what he said  but I felt like I was in command I felt  like I was actually doing science on my  own I had the thought processes down I  knew the statistical tricks I knew how  to get the data do the analysis how to  write it up and I kind of sat back  afterwards and I was like yeah I'm I'm a  scientist now it may be a bit late in  the day but it was an amazing feeling to  be like yeah this PhD has really changed  the way I think I felt very lucky  actually I felt very grateful to have  gone through the experience and to be  given this way of viewing the  and the tools and sort of tricks that  I've learned so basically that's where  I've been for the past a couple of  months mostly working on thesis stuff  completing Corrections resubmitting as a  standard practice for almost all PhDs  and then a few other sort of more fun  bits afterwards I just wanted to make  this quick video to update you on what  I've been doing and to shed a light on  those hidden steps at the end of the PhD  which no one seems to talk about but  yeah that's what I wanted to talk about  I'm really excited to be back making  videos doing the flying beast video was  a real labor of love it was a very long  progress I was checking for my emails I  actually started the progress with that  in January so very happy that that one's  out there loads more science videos that  are just waiting to be edited there are  some exciting collaborations coming up  and yeah I'm very very happy to be back  also a massive shout outs the people who  support me on patreon and ball with me  whilst I was await necessarily doing  other things thank you so much your  support does mean the world incidentally  if he found the fine beasts video  interesting I actually did a commentary  track so like a director's commentary on  how I made the video and sort of talking  about how he approached it the  philosophy of how I do science videos  there'll be a link in the description if  you're a patreon you can get it  automatically if you can't get it then  you can become a patreon which would  directly support what I do anyway that's  enough thank you very much for watching  you'll see me very soon and I'll see you  in the next one  you
 

